## 1. Assumptions surfaced (PAEOS-6)

Every assumption below is load-bearing and **unverified in this session** — `spec/` is absent, so none
could be checked against source. They are recorded so the executing worker can falsify them cheaply
rather than inherit them silently.

| # | Assumption | If false |
|---|---|---|
| A1 | SAEOS-1 §4.3 enumerates a *closed, finite* set of node types, each with a *named field list*. | Per-field scoring (§3) has no denominator; instrument needs redesign, not reparameterization. |
| A2 | §9/A8 asserts something of the form "sealed goals are losslessly (or near-losslessly) extractable into the §4.3 node graph". This is the proposition the probe falsifies. | The probe tests the wrong claim. **Worker must quote §9/A8 verbatim into the output before scoring** (§4, step 1). |
| A3 | "Sealed goal" is a determinate, queryable state — one can enumerate sealed goals and pick 3 without judgement calls. | Sample selection becomes a researcher-degree-of-freedom; pre-registration (§3.4) is void. |
| A4 | ≥3 sealed goals exist across `ledger/` + `reviews/tasks/`. | Probe cannot reach n=3; report `NO-GO` with reason `insufficient-corpus` rather than padding. |
| A5 | Extraction is judgeable per-field as a 3-valued verdict (extractable / partial / absent) by one reader. | Needs a second rater + agreement statistic; single-rater loss ratio is not defensible. |
| A6 | Loss ratio is intended as *unweighted* mean field fidelity. The objective does not define it. | Aggregate is meaningless if fields differ in criticality — mitigated by the veto rule (§3.3), which is why that rule exists. |
| A7 | The 3 goals should be *heterogeneous* (different shape/size/age), not 3 easy cases. | A homogeneous sample inflates fidelity and manufactures a GO. Selection rule pinned in §3.4. |

**A2 and A7 are the two that most plausibly silently invalidate the probe.** A2 because the probe can
faithfully measure the wrong proposition; A7 because it is the natural, unforced way an honest agent
produces a false GO.

