## 5. State machine (probe execution)

```
                  ┌─────────────┐
                  │   INIT      │
                  └──────┬──────┘
                         │ spec/ readable, §4.3 + §9/A8 located
                         ▼
                  ┌─────────────┐   §4.3 missing/open-ended (¬A1)
                  │ SCHEMA_LOAD ├──────────────────────────────────► ABORT(schema-undefined)
                  └──────┬──────┘
                         │ F built, |F| recorded
                         ▼
                  ┌─────────────┐   < 3 sealed goals (¬A4)
                  │ SELECT      ├──────────────────────────────────► NO-GO(insufficient-corpus)
                  └──────┬──────┘
                         │ G fixed and recorded — LOCKED
                         ▼
                  ┌─────────────┐
                  │ EXTRACT     │◄──┐ per cell
                  └──────┬──────┘   │
                         └──────────┘
                         │ all |G|×|F| cells filled
                         ▼
                  ┌─────────────┐
                  │ SCORE       │  pure; no judgement
                  └──────┬──────┘
                         ▼
                  ┌─────────────┐
                  │ VERDICT     │  apply §3.4 as written
                  └──┬───────┬──┘
            GO       │       │      NO-GO
                     ▼       ▼
                  ┌─────────────┐
                  │ EMIT        │ → reviews/spikes/sakg-s1-extraction-probe.md
                  └─────────────┘
```

Terminal states: `EMIT(GO)`, `EMIT(NO-GO)`, `ABORT(schema-undefined)`.

`ABORT` is distinct from `NO-GO`. NO-GO is a *result* — the probe ran and §9/A8 failed. ABORT means the
probe could not be run and carries **no** evidential weight about §9/A8. **This session is neither**: it
is a pre-INIT capability failure. Recording it as NO-GO would be a false falsification of §9/A8;
recording it as GO would be fabricated evidence.

Once `SELECT` completes, `G` is locked (§2, directionality).

